{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794924561,"nonce":"Vo5WVRM04aKvmsUM8FcEoRVWFDk=","title":"Found while validating c878f2a with hxn 0.3.7 and Haxe 4.3.6/hxcpp git on macOS arm64. A #[hxn::data] record field u32 with #[hxn(default = \"Options::default_rate\")] generates public var sampleRate:Null\u003cUInt\u003e = null and if (sampleRate == null) in hxnWrite. Passing {} works on HashLink/Ash, but hxcpp compiles the comparison to hx::IsEq((int)this-\u003esampleRate, null), turning absence into zero. The serializer writes present + zero, so the Rust default provider is skipped. A sample-rate default of 48000 arrives as zero and fails validation. Generated Rust correctly uses the default when the presence byte is false.","message":"Reproduce with an optional UInt record field, serialize an omitted value and an explicit zero under hxcpp/CPPIA, and distinguish them on Rust entry. Null checks must preserve absence without UInt's abstract comparison coercing the receiver to Int. Inspect optional UInt serialization too. Add a runtime regression, beyond generated-source assertions.\n\nThe first hxn-audio API uses signed Int for its bounded option counts/rates, which do not require UInt range. This keeps it usable with released hxn 0.3.7; it does not fix the generator defect. No source fix, commit or push made for this issue.","files":null}]}